(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_anatomyoffloweringplants=[{id:"neet-2026-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Bulliform cells",q:"The main function of bulliform cells in grasses is :",o:["to make the leaf impermeable to fungal spores.","to perform photosynthesis.","to minimize water loss during water stress.","to transport water."],c:2,s:`Bulliform cells are large, empty, colourless cells.
In water-scarce conditions they lose water and become flaccid.
Then the leaf curls inwards, which reduces the exposed surface area and so minimises water loss.`},{id:"neet-2026-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Tissues and cell types",q:`Match List-I with List-II :
List-I \u2014 List-II
A. Conjunctive tissue \u2014 I. Specialised cells in the vicinity of guard cells
B. Casparian strips \u2014 II. Endodermal cells rich in starch
C. Subsidiary cells \u2014 III. Tissue between xylem and phloem
D. Starch sheath \u2014 IV. Endodermal cells with suberin deposition`,o:["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],c:2,s:`A. Conjunctive tissue is the tissue between xylem and phloem.
B. Casparian strips are found in endodermal cells; they are suberin depositions in the cell wall.
C. Subsidiary cells are specialised cells in the vicinity of guard cells.
D. Starch sheath is another name for endodermal cells rich in starch.`},{id:"neet-2025-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Monocot stem",q:"Find the statement that is NOT correct with regard to the structure of monocot stem.",o:["Hypodermis is parenchymatous.","Vascular bundles are scattered.","Vascular bundles are conjoint and closed.","Phloem parenchyma is absent."],c:0,s:"In monocot stem, the hypodermis is sclerenchymatous, not parenchymatous."},{id:"neet-2024-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Bulliform cells in monocot leaf",q:"Builiform cells are responsible for",o:["Increased photosynthesis in monocots","Providing large spaces for storage of sugar","Inward curling of leaves in monocots","Protecting the plant from salt stress"],c:2,s:`In grasses, some adaxial epidermal cells along the veins become large, empty, colourless cells called bulliform cells.
When they are turgid (full of water), the leaf surface is exposed.
When they are flaccid due to water stress, they make the leaf curl inwards, which reduces water loss.`},{id:"neet-2024-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Permanent tissues",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Parenchyma is living but collenchyma is dead tissue
Statement II: Gymnosperms lack xylem vessels but presence of xylem vessels is the characteristic of angiosperms.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],c:1,s:`Collenchyma is also a living tissue, so Statement I is false.
Gymnosperms lack xylem vessels, and vessels are a feature of angiosperms, so Statement II is true.`},{id:"neet-2023-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Secondary growth: annual rings",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : Late wood has fewer xylary elements with narrow vessels.
Reason R : Cambium is less active in winters
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],c:1,s:`In winter the cambium is less active.
It then forms fewer xylary elements with narrow vessels.
This wood is called autumn wood or late wood.
So both are true and R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2023-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Xylem: endarch and exarch",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Endarch and exarch are the terms often used for describing the position of secondary xylem in the plant body.
Statement II : Exarch condition is the most common feature of the root system.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct but Statement II is false"],c:0,s:`Endarch and exarch describe the position of primary xylem, not secondary xylem. So Statement I is false.
Primary xylem has protoxylem and metaxylem. Their relative position gives the endarch or exarch arrangement.
Exarch xylem is seen in roots, so Statement II is true.`},{id:"neet-2023-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Secondary growth bark",q:`Identify the correct statements:
A. Lenticels are the lens-shaped openings permitting the exchange of gases.
B. Bark formed early in the season is called hard bark.
C. Bark is a technical term that refers to all tissues exterior to vascular cambium.
D. Bark refers to periderm and secondary phloem.
E. Phellogen is single-layered in thickness.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and C only","B, C and E only","A and D only","A, B and D only"],c:2,s:`A is correct: lenticels are lens-shaped openings for gas exchange.
B is wrong: bark formed early in the season is early or soft bark; late bark is hard.
C is wrong: bark is a non-technical term for all tissues outside the vascular cambium.
D is correct: bark includes periderm and secondary phloem.
E is wrong: phellogen is a couple of layers thick.
So only A and D are correct.`}]})();
